<a href="#"><img alt="P2" src="https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/p2.svg?v=9" align="top"></a> **Receiver import failures look green**

`_compose_with_installed_handler` silently switches to the local parser copy when an installed Agent fails to import. `test_receiver_contract_matches_installed_agent` only compares accepted effort names, so it cannot catch changed parsing behavior or a broken import with the same names. The tests can pass without checking the installed receiver.

Keep the fallback for absent installations, but fail or clearly skip the receiver checks when an installation is found and cannot run.

Note: If this suggestion doesn't match your team's coding style, reply to this and let me know. I'll remember it for next time!

=== chatgpt-codex-connector[bot] 2026-10-14T12:17:48Z api/runner_client.py:81
**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Keep runner capability probes on the no-redirect client**

When `runner-local` starts a run with `max` (or another non-legacy effort), this forwards the runner bearer token into `_gateway_run_model_options`, whose capability path calls `get_gateway_caps`; that function uses global `urllib.request.urlopen` rather than `HttpRunnerClient._opener()`. A 3xx from `/v1/capabilities` is therefore followed with `Authorization` copied to the redirected request, bypassing the no-redirect guarantee already enforced for every other runner call and potentially disclosing `HERMES_WEBUI_RUNNER_API_KEY`; perform the probe through the hardened opener (or omit the credential) instead.

