<!-- automation-artifact-cleanup-2026-10-15:start -->
## Branch hygiene follow-up — remove automation artifacts (2026-10-15)

Commit `dd2fac6f8eefc377b6e75f59c6e9dc1121173e95` removes four workflow-local files accidentally committed after the LM Studio regression coverage: `AGENT_CONTEXT.json`, `AGENT_RESULT.json`, `agent.out`, and `gate.out`.

Verification:

- The resulting Git tree is exactly `1276014c546baa6d17b720d162d19c4455d71d08`, identical to the clean, tested tree from `c3d31745e4c52b929b40d02fdeb87b413348beb1`.
- The LM Studio boundary suite recorded for that tree: **30 passed, 1 skipped**.
- Exact-head CI for cleanup commit `dd2fac6f8eefc377b6e75f59c6e9dc1121173e95` is fully green: **Tests**, **Browser smoke**, **Docs CI**, and **Conversation lifecycle** all completed successfully.
<!-- automation-artifact-cleanup-2026-10-15:end -->

<!-- lmstudio-snapshot-probe-2026-10-15:start -->
## Maintainer re-gate follow-up — LM Studio probe snapshot isolation (2026-10-15)

Addressed the final finding from the re-gate of `b044e2a91e3b` ([review #5523470304](https://github.com/nesquena/hermes-webui/pull/8241#pullrequestreview-5523470304)) in commit `3de670dcedbd2b9681b01a8de5f8eb16fdbb27a2`:

- Explicit LM Studio reasoning probes now resolve both the configured endpoint and API key from the request's lock-protected profile snapshot.
- Legacy callers that do not supply a snapshot retain their historical global-cache call shape.
- A two-thread regression pauses each request after explicit-provider selection, reloads the process-global cache to another profile, and proves both Gateway and runner lanes probe only the analyst endpoint/key and retain `high` instead of degrading to `low`.

Verification on the exact uploaded tree `6876150ca3f405900ce1050c2459e6e2df1fec0b`:

- Gateway/runner model-options boundary: **27 passed, 1 skipped**.
- Adjacent LM Studio/reasoning suites: **87 passed, 1 skipped**.
- Changed-test Ruff, Python compilation, and `git diff --check`: clean.
- Whole-file Ruff for `api/config.py` still reports its three pre-existing findings (unused `traceback`, unused `parse_qs`, duplicate `collections` import); none are in this diff.

Exact-head CI is fully green: **Tests**, **Browser smoke**, **Docs CI**, and **Conversation lifecycle** all completed successfully. Maintainer/review-bot re-gate remains pending.
<!-- lmstudio-snapshot-probe-2026-10-15:end -->

<!-- snapshot-provider-residual-2026-10-15:start -->
## Re-push check follow-up — omitted-provider snapshot resolution (2026-10-15)

Addressed the remaining portion of the snapshot finding from the scoped re-push check at `a52b8779` ([comment #6175331457](https://github.com/nesquena/hermes-webui/pull/8241#issuecomment-6175331457)) in commit `b044e2a91e3b9da00538252bb630b5db09494b1e`:

- **Provider resolution off the snapshot:** `resolve_model_provider()` now accepts the caller's captured config snapshot and reads every routing input from it — the configured default provider, `custom_providers[]` ownership, `providers[]` entries, aliases and endpoint lookups — preserving explicit model hints and the existing routing rules. Without a snapshot, behaviour and call shapes are unchanged.
- **Helper coverage:** `_get_provider_base_url()`, `_get_providers_cfg()`, `_get_provider_cfg()` and `_model_id_declared_in_config()` take the same optional snapshot; the legacy zero-arg call shape is kept on the no-snapshot path so strict-signature stand-ins keep working.
- **Coercion path:** `_resolve_model_reasoning_efforts_impl()` resolves the omitted provider through the snapshot instead of the global resolver — a concurrent default-profile reload can no longer swap the provider and degrade `high` to `low` in the replayed two-thread scenario.
- **Tests:** omitted- and explicit-provider capture → cross-profile reload → coercion cases for the Gateway and runner lanes. RED on `c5b2cc5e`: the omitted case resolves `low` (reproducing the replay); both cases pass after.

Verification on the exact pushed head `b044e2a91e3b9da00538252bb630b5db09494b1e`:

- Boundary file + resolver battery in three groups: **389 passed**, **143 passed**, **200 passed**; Gateway approval file: **77 passed**.
- Ruff on the changed file reports the same three pre-existing findings as base `c5b2cc5e`; no new findings introduced. `py_compile` and `git diff --check`: clean.
<!-- snapshot-provider-residual-2026-10-15:end -->

<!-- ci-followup-2026-10-15:start -->
## CI follow-up — `c5b2cc5e` (Tests-matrix fallout from `a52b8779`)

The first Tests run on `a52b8779` was red in 5 of 15 shards. Two of those were on me, and both are fixed in `c5b2cc5e`:

- **Strict-signature compatibility:** `resolve_model_reasoning_efforts()` / `coerce_reasoning_effort_for_model()` now pass `config_data` down only when a snapshot was actually captured. The unconditional keyword broke a strict-signature monkeypatch stand-in in `test_issue3958_reasoning_post_session_context.py` (shard 1 of all three Python versions).
- **Race-test scheduling:** the concurrent default-profile reader in the boundary test now yields outside the cache lock, so the request thread can't be starved on slow runners (this was the >60 s pytest-timeout in the 3.11/3.12 shard-2 jobs).

For transparency on the other three shard-1 jobs in that run: they ended early without a pytest summary (silent stop at ~54 %, right after `test_issue7542`). The same signature also shows on current `master` and on an unrelated branch, and it doesn't reproduce locally with the full shard-1 suite under `--timeout=60` — flagging in case it's a known runner-side pattern.

Local verification on `c5b2cc5e`: all four re-gate pins red→green; focused reasoning/Gateway/runner set **440 passed, 9 skipped**; full shard 1 completes (**4154 passed, 85 skipped**; only a local-environment TLS-probe artifact fails here); ruff: **0 new findings on changed lines** vs base `adc5dad9`.
<!-- ci-followup-2026-10-15:end -->


